my/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-47552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/2c633342d5dc1ddb1244d492d81b6b9c00ffe33858398a72acb2a73467d93bb0.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=2$ and $x=4.$*\n\n${g}^{′}(4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=4.$ Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4)-g(4-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(4)-g(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-343816" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343816" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/894d659f3ef4544eeda653f5e098d9ca26f3e06899798441534a1bf395628d0e.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3)-f(3-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(3)-f(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-72074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/5773f1515892d8eb2cb77c700eab48f7cf44b0f91e9e157960798574e23ca93d.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x)-g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2.$ . Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=1$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2)-g(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2)-g(1)}{1} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-47547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-51328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The altitude $h(t)$ of a falling object object, measured in meters, is recorded every second for an interval of six seconds. The values of $h(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $h(t)$ | $200$ | $195$ | $181$ | $159$ | $128$ | $91$ | $51$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the altitude when $t=3$ using a backward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-51328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{h(t)-h(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=3.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{h(3)-h(3-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{h(3)-h(2)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{159-181}{1} \\\\  & =-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of change of the altitude is $-22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-51328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-51328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-51328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "-26\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "-31\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "21\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "38\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-51328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-22\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The number of deer in a wildlife reserve, measured in hundreds, is given by the function $D(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in years, since the study began. The values of $D(t)$ for several values of $t$ are shown in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $D(t)$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ | $14$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the deer population when $t=4$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is\n{{field-1}} hundreds per year." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{D(t)-D(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=4.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{D(4)-D(4-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(4)-D(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-14}{1} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=4$ is $5$ hundreds per year." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-343833" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343833" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A baseball is hit by a batter. The distance between the batter and the ball, measured in miles, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $0.015$ | $0.03$ | $0.045$ | $0.05$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity ${s}^{′}(t)$ when $t=1$ using a backward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t)-s(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{s(1)-s(1-0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1)-s(0.5)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.03-0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.015}{0.5} \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is $0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47485" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-47485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.015\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "0.025\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "0.02\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}"] ["math" "0.05\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47485" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.03\\,\\text{mi}/\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An oil tank is leaking. The volume of oil in the tank, in liters, after $t$ hours is given by the function $q(t).$ The values of $q(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)$ | $200$ | $192$ | $186$ | $182$ | $180$ |\n\nApproximate the rate at which the volume of oil in the tank is changing when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =186-192 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of change of the volume is $-6$ liters per hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-72075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$-4$ liters per hour"] ["text" "$-6$ liters per hour"] ["text" "$4$ liters per hour"] ["text" "$5$ liters per hour"] ["text" "$6$ liters per hour"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-72075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$-6$ liters per hour"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The battery charge of a delivery drone, measured in percent, is given by the function $B(t)$, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the drone was fully charged. The values of $B(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B(t)$ | $80$ | $72$ | $55$ | $40$ | $35$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of the battery charge when $t=6$ using a backward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is\n{{field-1}} percent per hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{B}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{B(t)-B(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{B(6)-B(6-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{B(6)-B(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-55}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $t=6$ is $-\\frac{15}{2}$ percent per hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-343841" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343841" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/3a4526a6979a86aa924bddca54b05778e91bb67a1dcb6159c5436cc975b22668.png)\n\nA cyclist records the total distance traveled after $x$ minutes. The total distance traveled after $x$ minutes, in miles, is given by the function $D(x).$ The graph of $D(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=45$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=15.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=30$ and $x=45.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is\n{{field-1}} miles per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{D}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{D(x)-D(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=45.$  Applying the above formula with $x=45$ and $h=15$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{′}(45) & ≈\\frac{D(45)-D(45-15)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{D(45)-D(30)}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{11-6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=45$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ miles per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-343858" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343858" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-86484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/1fcdf0d68d78cba445d1e3ecfeec237ee4e33d7c74cb3b292593fe8b717fe6b8.png)\n\nPeter notices that the total number of eggs produced on his farm $t$ weeks after the beginning of his observations is given by the function $N(t).$ The graph of $N(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the number of produced eggs increases when $t=5$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-86484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{N(t)-N(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=5.$ Applying the above formula with $t=5$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{N(5)-N(5-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(5)-N(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{800-600}{1} \\\\  & =200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the rate of increase is $200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-86484" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-86484" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-86484" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "100\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}"] ["math" "200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}"] ["math" "600\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}"] ["math" "800\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}"] ["math" "900\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-86484" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "200\\,\\text{eggs}/\\text{week}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/9a5fa5cdd4734813d18af5b99e3869051f15e137f72e98ad5529c751e6f85d87.png)\n\nA model rocket’s height is recorded during a launch. The height after $x$ seconds, in meters, is given by the function $H(x).$ The graph of $H(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=12.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=4$ and $x=16.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is\n{{field-1}} meters per second." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{H}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{H(x)-H(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=16.$  Applying the above formula with $x=16$ and $h=12$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{H(16)-H(16-12)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(16)-H(4)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=16$ is $\\frac{1}{6}$ meters per second." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-343862" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343862" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/64e35c559b233a28c5913ffa37ad949653ad2b46a0e10db639626b8575b7a263.png)\n\nAn oil tank is leaking $q(t)$ liters of oil, where $t>0$ is the time in hours since the leak started.\n The graph of $q(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{q(t)-q(t-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=2.$ Applying the above formula with $t=2$ and $h=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{q(2)-q(2-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1}{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the rate of leaking is $2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47357" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-47357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "1.5\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "1\\,\\text{liter}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] ["math" "3\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-47357" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\,\\text{liters}/\\text{hour}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/fd917228308b839be77110b07e32fa0154350c290920628400bd1f20a34160b5.png)\n\nA factory tracks the number of defective items remaining after a recall. The number of defective items after $x$ days, in hundreds, is given by the function $N(x).$ The graph of $N(x)$ is shown above.  \n\nApproximate the rate of change when $x=15$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=10$ and $x=15.$*\n\nThe estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is\n{{field-1}} hundred items per day." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{N}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{N(x)-N(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=15.$  Applying the above formula with $x=15$ and $h=5$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{′}(15) & ≈\\frac{N(15)-N(15-5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{N(15)-N(10)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{72-76}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the estimated rate of change when $x=15$ is $-\\frac{4}{5}$ hundred items per day." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343869" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0ab0127b09326b9480bb2e64a6004f0af49145c47f990fb6b62f8576df40bcd6"} {"question" "q-343869" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "q-343869" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/state.json" "file_sha256" "dbf6bf149064828b1103a4eaae2d31d5202af35460c979b14b79804719118946"} {"question" "e-155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-155.json" "file_sha256" "c902ab54bf1d693cad248bb1af4f1fd89e8ebc4ebc3bb96e1a20fc5eb9229464"} {"question" "e-155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=16.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(16) & ≈\\frac{f(16)-f(16-3)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(16)-f(13)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{21-15}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-155.json" "file_sha256" "c902ab54bf1d693cad248bb1af4f1fd89e8ebc4ebc3bb96e1a20fc5eb9229464"} {"question" "e-6104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/6e3f77e5b7d491e3ee43c8f8150065282d2abc259e3a88751b93ff15b27f492a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-6104.json" "file_sha256" "0ced31f92be32d2adb21ed3d01a2eab7de9274dbcfa64800377b9d2a793c89f3"} {"question" "e-6104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x) - g(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=6.$ Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{g(6)-g(6-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-6104.json" "file_sha256" "0ced31f92be32d2adb21ed3d01a2eab7de9274dbcfa64800377b9d2a793c89f3"} {"question" "e-156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-156.json" "file_sha256" "6c05a46f8a211f91bad745da6130787f0a3ee0eee6d98545cb81f2fe83c6c0f3"} {"question" "e-156" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t) - s(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1.6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(1.6) & ≈\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.6-0.2)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(1.6)-s(1.4)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.9-2.7}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.2}{0.2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at the time $t=1.6,$ the velocity is approximately $1\\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-156.json" "file_sha256" "6c05a46f8a211f91bad745da6130787f0a3ee0eee6d98545cb81f2fe83c6c0f3"} {"question" "e-6105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A factory produces fans. The total cost of producing $x$ fans, in dollars, is given by the function $C(x).$  The graph of $C(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/assets/ad3fae3694c4df8269be9a54350aabf83368b3152ea69eb2b9d1ac76e702823f.png)\n\nApproximate the marginal cost $C'(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-6105.json" "file_sha256" "eb8c3d8b1f196ea478ca837677a48a328bf7262f21b73abbcefc9a6cdb372096"} {"question" "e-6105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nC'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{C(x) - C(x-h)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{C(3)-C(3-2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{C(3)-C(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30-20}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the marginal cost is $\\[math]5$ per fan." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058551/example-6105.json" "file_sha256" "eb8c3d8b1f196ea478ca837677a48a328bf7262f21b73abbcefc9a6cdb372096"}] "usage" {"e-6104" [] "e-6105" [] "e-155" [] "e-156" [] "q-47547" []} "basis" 1872 "retractions" [[17592186092677 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/q-47547.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g^{′}(x)$ at the point $x = 2$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h = 1$."] [17592186091906 :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ | $13$ | $16$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-12$ | $-7$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $15$ | $21$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=16$ using a backward difference approximation."] [17592186091906 :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x) - f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=16$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=3$. This gives\n\n$$\nf^{′}(16) \\mid \\approx (f(16) - f(16 - 3))/(3); = (f(16) - f(13))/(3); = \\frac{21 - 15}{3}; = \\frac{6}{3}; = 2\n$$"] [17592186092031 :question/problem "The graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=6$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=4$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/ae0cc73bdba85ba6e93ee3fd020172bd.png)"] [17592186092031 :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x) - g(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=6$. Applying the above formula with $x=6$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\ng^{′}(6) \\mid \\approx (g(6) - g(6 - 4))/(4); = (g(6) - g(2))/(4); = \\frac{6 - 4}{4}; = \\frac{2}{4}; = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$"] [17592186091908 :question/problem "A race car's position along a straight track, measured in meters from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ | $1.6$ | $1.8$ | $2.0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $s(t)$ | $1.3$ | $2.7$ | $2.9$ | $3.5$ | $5.0$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ when $t=1.6$ using a backward difference approximation."] [17592186091908 :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t) - s(t-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=1.6$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2$. This gives\n\n$$\ns^{′}(1.6) \\mid \\approx (s(1.6) - s(1.6 - 0.2))/(0.2); = (s(1.6) - s(1.4))/(0.2); = \\frac{2.9 - 2.7}{0.2}; = \\frac{0.2}{0.2}; = 1\n$$\n\nSo at the time $t=1.6$, the velocity is approximately $1\\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}$."] [17592186092032 :question/problem "A factory produces fans. The total cost of producing $x$ fans, in dollars, is given by the function $C(x)$. The graph of $C(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/993/Source/Images/5709d3ae0923e76a3e63bf4238582d36.png)\n\nApproximate the marginal cost $C'(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a backward difference approximation with step size $h=2$."] [17592186092032 :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nC'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{C(x) - C(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$. Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\nC^{′}(3) \\mid \\approx (C(3) - C(3 - 2))/(2); = (C(3) - C(1))/(2); = \\frac{30 - 20}{2}; = \\frac{10}{2}; = 5\n$$\n\nSo the marginal cost is $\\[math]5$ per fan."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186090528 17592186090529 17592186090530 17592186090531 17592186090532] "immutable_fields" [[17592186090527 :answer-field/correct] [17592186090527 :answer-field/id] [17592186090527 :answer-field/key] [17592186090527 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
